 the poor and the sick I  thought this is a way that I can bring  my faith into my work place and not  shove it down people's throat I don't  have to tell anybody all i have to do is  just do it and hopefully my faith will  speak a voice of its own so i began to  travel and eventually I became chairman  of the committee and of the whole  committee and that included domestic  hunger and an international hunger and I  ended up going to about 130 nations  traveling so many different places we  legislated passed a lot of laws that are  in that have been in law for a number of  years and and  what happened was that I became so  involved in this issue that I began to  get letters and phone calls from my  district in Dayton Ohio saying you know  congressman we think that you think more  about Ethiopia or Africa or Asia than  you do about they know how who needs you  you need to come home and think about  Dayton Ohio there was a perception that  I was thinking more international than I  was my own district it wasn't a true  perception but in politics perception is  a lot and people have a perception about  you it's you either better change it or  you're going to get stuck with that  perception so I got my my staff together  and I said look there's this perception  we need to change it I love this issue I  don't want to lose this issue because I  could lose my office here I said let's  let's bring this issue back home to Ohio  and let's let's educate my constituency  let's do some things back here and let's  find out what's going on here in Dayton  Ohio and the suburbs and things in South  in southwest Ohio which we did and we  started a gleaning program you know a  gleaning program is a is you know where  you go into the fields and you pick the  produce that's not picked by farmers you  know there's a hundred and ten million  tons of food every year that goes to  waste in this country 110 million tons  it wastes in the fields it doesn't get  picked or it wastes and grocery stores  are on the way to the wholesaler or we  wasted in our own homes we have to throw  it out 110 million tons so we decided  through churches and various Jewish  groups secular groups that we would go  out at harvest time and we would find  some farmers that really wanted to help  us and we had all we had so much food  it was unbelievable and we did this for  about 15 years we had gleaning programs  at harvest time that picked up fruits  and vegetables and just had so much food  we didn't know what to do with it but we  had a system where we gathered it and  took took it to a central warehouse and  I had 66 food banks and soup kitchens  than the word went out today we got  strawberries tomorrow we got apples now  we got whatever whatever was corn  whatever was being harvested in Ohio at  the time which was a lot of food and  then we started as senior citizen  feeding program on the weekends during  the cold winter months when a lot of  them are shut in that lasted about 20  years and then we did a immunization  program for kids and we did a pickup of  food programs from country clubs and  rest in restaurants and motels and and I  had a huge military base in my in my  backyard that we gathered and we got the  people involved because I felt that I  had to bring the issue back home because  I felt it's incumbent upon elected  officials to educate your district you  know sometimes elected a lectin  officials you know not sometimes they  should lead and not wait for the wind to  change it's a coming upon them to lead  and to educate and to and to direct  prior what I had first got elected I did  a poll in my district because I wanted  to know how my people felt about issues  I said you know so we listed a number of  issues the deficit they jobs whatever  the issues were in those days the budget  taxes environment etc poverty hunger and  I said ranked his issues well hunger  poverty ranked glass out of 15 I think  it was 15 or 20 issues ranked last we  did these programs and before I left  office after 24 years I did this pole  again same poll asked the same questions  hunger ranked number two so you can  change your constituents minds and you  can change for good and you can bring an  issue back home and make things happen  and that was really very very exciting  to me I right now I run up nonprofit  organization in Washington DC it's  called the Alliance to end hunger and  it's a little organization we're growing  and what we do is we build political  will we do a number of things we I  believe that hunger poverty development  assistance but especially the issue of  hunger the only way to solve it if  you've got to bring everybody into the  room it's not just government this  belongs to private enterprise it belongs  to the people of faith and it belongs to  the nonprofit's so our organization has  corporations large corporations from  cargill to land of lakes to other  corporations to the Catholic Relief  Services to world vision to save the  children to and then on the religious  side the faith-based we have the Islamic  Relief a lot of Christians are Christian  churches and two major Jewish  organizations so we bring everybody into  the room and we work on creating  political will we do that in a number of  ways we do polling we work on  corporations meet working with nonprofit  organizations overseas in our own  country we we build national alliances  in other countries especially in poor  nations you know I spent a lot of time  with congressmen and Senators trying to  get them to do things to be better men  and women for this issue talking to them  about this issue sometimes lobbying them  and we're doing what's going to start  doing the same thing in other countries  in poor nations because working with  civil society it's you know some of the  some of the civil society and very poor  nations for them to learn how to lobby  and for them to be advocates is  something very new to them so we're  teaching them we're teaching civil  society how to how to advocate and then  the other thing I'm trying to reconnect  and we've started this congressmen and  senators and governors and mayors back  to their district about hunger so they  can have hunger free communities and I'm  sick and tired of elected officials just  running to be reelected I want them to  be leaders again I want them to be  interested in this issue this issue is  if it's if it's not in the top five  issues of our world today I don't know  what isn't and as we pull on this issue  it's it's it's one of the top two or  three issues always with the American  people the American people are way ahead  of our elected officials on this issue  so what I'm going to do or what I am  trying to do is get the the congressmen  and Senators to go back to the district  get acquainted with their food bank  people their faith based people there  their corporations and call a meeting  and we have a 14-point program that we  really started back when I was a  congressman and chairman of the  committee that we are asking them to in  some districts around the country to  start doing hunger-free communities that  what would it take to make your district  hunger-free and if you could be able to  say to your people that nobody's going  to go to bed hungry again in my district  and you imagine what that would do for  the morale the economics I think people  would want to live there I think  corporal races would want to invest  there so we're going to  we're going to take congressmen senators  and Mara get them reacquainted with  their district especially the hunger  areas and we're trying to build hunger  heroes that's pretty much what the  alliance to end hunger does I'm the  executive director of it and that's what  i do now in conclusion I just want to  say that there's a lot of overwhelming  things going on in the world today when  it comes to hunger and poverty but I had  the great chance to be with Mother  Teresa number of times in my lifetime I  even went to her funeral but I remember  the first time I met her in Calcutta  first when I met her she grabbed my hand  and she said five things to me it was  pretty much what miroslav said she  quoted right for Matthew 25 she said  never forget never forget she grabbed  each one of my fingers for the least of  these for the least of these that's five  that's a five words she said to me then  she took me downtown in Calcutta we went  to this place I can't remember exactly  where it was but it was in such a  congested part of Calcutta that people  were living on the sidewalk and sleeping  on the street I mean it just it was  overrun with people and I'm thinking I'm  chairman of the Select Committee and  hunger and I must have blurted out  something to her to Mother Teresa I said  look at this I mean I'm supposed to be  chairman of the hunger committee I'm not  even sure I can handle this block with  all these people here she said where do  you start and she she didn't say  anything and she went and there was a  man lying in the street she got some men  to pick him up take him home to her to  her  hospice like place and she began to wash  him off and love him and hug him like  she always does and she came back to me  later in the afternoon and she said kind  of task answer my question she said do  the thing it's in front of you she said  you can't always not everybody like you  can come to Calcutta but if everybody  did the things in front of him what's  going on in New Haven what's going on in  Boise Idaho what's going on in dayton  ohio if everybody did the thing that's  in front of do the thing it's in front  of you probably half the stuff we do in  Congress who wouldn't need so that was  her point to me and that's my point to  you just do the thing that's in front of  you okay good to talk to you
 

